  "answer": "Myopia, or near-sightedness, is a condition where a person can see nearby objects clearly but cannot see distant objects clearly. This happens because the image of distant objects is formed in front of the retina instead of on it. To correct myopia, a concave lens is used. This lens diverges the light rays before they enter the eye, allowing the image to form on the retina. The focal length of the lens is chosen such that it forms an image of a distant object at the far point of the person's eye. A ray diagram would show parallel rays from a distant object converging after passing through the concave lens to form an image at the far point, which then acts as an object for the eye lens, resulting in a clear image on the retina.",
